    'Active management of the third stage reduces PPH: a uterotonic after delivery of the baby, controlled cord traction (by trained attendants), and uterine tone assessment; delayed cord clamping (1–3 minutes) is recommended. Oxytocin 10 IU IM or IV is the uterotonic of choice. Where oxytocin is unavailable or its quality cannot be guaranteed (cold-chain problems), heat-stable carbetocin or oral misoprostol (600 µg) are alternatives; ergometrine is avoided in hypertension and heart disease. Tranexamic acid within 3 hours of birth is given for established PPH (WOMAN trial).',
